# HIT_ANY_CHAR — Project 56, the first case.
#
# Sites are the `v` itself (DESIGN §7.4): `v` names are qualified by addrbook
# entry and derived from the source, so no IR position is ever named and this
# file does not become write-once when anything upstream changes.
#
# `frame+N` rather than an absolute: it is the spelling HIT_ANY_CHAR.c's own
# frame comment uses ("ch at slot 2", "length word at 4"), so the oracle is
# checkable by eye against the C and against the disassembly, and the addrbook
# stays the single source of the base.

routine HIT_ANY_CHAR

# --- placement -------------------------------------------------------------
place  v0            frame+2        # ch, CHAR(1); the even pair 2-3 (Salvage F22)
place  v2            frame+4        # the 30-byte varying temp
place  v4            frame+4        # the SAME temp — merge; live ranges checked
place  v1            0x7016DE07:0   # the literal, in the code image
# NOT a placement: `GET_INPUT.a1` was on this list until the monotonicity
# replay showed the line moving the distance by 0.  R2 already derives the
# argument cell (0x74003F1C = wfp-10-2N) from the addrbook, so supplying it
# was supplying something the model explains.  Oracle length 5 -> 4.

# --- rewrites --------------------------------------------------------------
# "CHAR constant <= 2 bytes -> WLDAI + one wide store" — named as future work
# by HIT_ANY_CHAR.c's own header before any of this was measured.
rewrite pack_varying_imm  at=v4  reg=ac1
